1
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:46,840
CONVERSATION 1
Today our guest is Rosie Melinda who works as a features editor for Fashion magazine.

2
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:58,000
Hi Rosie, you’re a features editor at one of the most widely read women’s magazines in the U.K. What kind of responsibilities does that job entail?

3
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:07,000
We spend our days looking at ideas from journalists, writing copy for the magazine and website and editing.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,420
We do random things like asking people in the street questions and testing sports clothing.

5
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:18,880
We also do less tangible things like understanding what our readers want.

6
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:21,560
It’s certainly varied and sometimes bizarre.

7
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:27,620
During your working day what kind of work might you typically do?

8
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:36,980
My day mainly incorporates responding to emails, writing and editing stories and coming up with new feature ideas.

9
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:43,440
How does the job of features editor differ from that of fashion editor or other editorial positions?

10
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:51,240
The feature’s team deals with articles such as careers, reports, confidence and confessions.

11
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,260
Everything except to fashion and beauty.

12
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:59,300
A lot of people believe that working at a magazine is a glamorous job.

13
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,380
Is this an accurate representation of what you do?

14
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:09,180
I’d say it’s glamorous to an extent, but not in the way it’s portrayed in films.

15
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:16,820
We do have our moments such as interviewing celebrities and attending parties which is a huge thrill.

16
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:19,500
Ultimately though, we’re the same as our readers.

17
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,780
But working in a job we’re all very lucky to have.

18
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:28,520
Did you have to overcome any difficulties to reach this point in your career?

19
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:30,700
How did you manage to do this?

20
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:35,660
I had to be really persistent and it was very hard work.

21
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:44,540
After three years of working in a petrol station and doing unpaid work I still hadn’t managed to get an entry level job.

22
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:49,120
I was lucky that my last desperate attempt led to a job.

23
00:02:49,120 --> 00:03:04,080
I told myself that all experiences make you a better journalist in the long run and luckily, I was right.

24
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:20,440
Q1. What is the woman’s profession?

25
00:03:20,440 --> 00:03:37,620
Q2. What is one of the woman’s main responsibilities?

26
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:56,140
Q3. What do many people think about the woman’s job?

27
00:03:56,140 --> 00:04:13,660
Q4. What helped the woman to get her current position?

28
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:18,380
CONVERSATION 2
Are you watching any good shows these days?

29
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:19,740
Actually, yes.

30
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:22,740
I’m watching a great satire called Frankie.

31
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:24,440
I think you’d like it.

32
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:25,660
Really?

33
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:27,200
What’s it about?

34
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,180
It’s about a real guy named Frankie.

35
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:38,660
He is a famous comedian in New York and shows a mixture of comedy and drama loosely depicting his life.

36
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:44,600
I’m sorry, do you mean to say, it’s a real-life series about a real person?

37
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,560
It’s non-fiction, isn’t it?

38
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:53,420
No … Not really, no. It’s fiction, as what happens in every episode is made up.

39
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:59,040
However the lead role is a comedian by the name of Frankie, and he plays himself.

40
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:09,280
So Frankie in both real life and in the TV show lives in New York City, is a comic, is divorced, and has two little daughters.

41
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:16,720
All those things are true, but aside from him, all his friends and family are played by actors.

42
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:20,740
And the plots and the events that take place are also invented.

43
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:23,280
Oh, I think I see now.

44
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:26,680
That sounds like a very original concept.

45
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:27,940
Yes, it is.

46
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:37,600
In fact, the whole show is written, directed, edited and produced by him, and is very funny and has won many awards.

47
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:38,260
That’s cool.

48
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:40,540
I will try to download it.

49
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:43,740
I’m watching a comedy called The Big Bang Theory.

50
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:45,480
It’s a huge hit around the world.

51
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:46,880
Oh yes.

52
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:49,560
I’ve heard of it, but never actually watched it.

53
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:52,200
Well, then you should check it out.

54
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:54,100
It’s also very funny.

55
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:58,200
It’s about four male scientists and a female waitress.

56
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,720
The men are very socially awkward but very bright.

57
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:07,020
And this is contrasted by the lady’s social skills and common sense.

58
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:13,060
The show has been running for over ten years, and some of the actors are practically global super stars.

59
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:23,120
Now that they are such famous celebrities.

60
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:42,160
Q5. What does the man think of the satire Frankie he recently watched?

61
00:06:42,160 --> 00:07:01,260
Q6. What does the man say especial about the satire Frankie?

62
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:20,480
Q7. What does the woman say she is going to do with the satire Frankie?

63
00:07:20,480 --> 00:08:09,770
Q8. What does the woman say about the comedy The Big Bang Theory?

64
00:08:09,770 --> 00:08:15,070
PASSAGE 1
Related to the ‘use-it-or-lose-it law’ is the ‘keep-moving principle’.

65
00:08:15,070 --> 00:08:18,090
We learned about stagnation from nature.

66
00:08:18,090 --> 00:08:21,350
A river that stops moving gets smelly.

67
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:27,070
The same thing happens to people who stop moving, either mentally or physically.

68
00:08:27,070 --> 00:08:34,550
Those who play contact sports know that the player who usually gets hurt the most is the one who is standing still.

69
00:08:34,550 --> 00:08:45,090
Of course, you’ll need some time to catch your breath every so often, but the essential massage is keep moving, extending and learning.

70
00:08:45,090 --> 00:08:51,530
Ships last a lot longer when they go to sea than when they stay in the harbor.

71
00:08:51,530 --> 00:08:53,390
The same is true for the airplanes.

72
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:59,970
You don’t preserve an airplane by keeping it on the ground, you preserve it by keeping it in service.

73
00:08:59,970 --> 00:09:05,470
We also get to live a long healthy life by staying in service.

74
00:09:05,470 --> 00:09:13,010
Longevity statistics reveal that the average person doesn’t last very long after retirement.

75
00:09:13,010 --> 00:09:16,150
The more we’ll hear is don’t retire.

76
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:28,670
If a fellow says “I’m 94 years old and I worked all my life”, we need to realize, that is how he got to be 94, by staying involved.

77
00:09:28,670 --> 00:09:34,150
George Bernard Shaw won a Nobel Prize when he was nearly 70.

78
00:09:34,150 --> 00:09:45,630
Benjamin Franklin produced some of his best writings at the age of 84, and Pablo Picasso put brush to canvas right through his eighties.

79
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:48,350
Isn’t the issue how old we think we are?

80
00:09:48,350 --> 00:09:55,410
A bonus with the ‘keep-moving principle’ is that while we keep moving, we don’t have a chance to worry.

81
00:09:55,410 --> 00:10:07,690
Hence we avoid the dreaded paralyses by analysis.

82
00:10:07,690 --> 00:10:26,630
Q9. What does the speaker say about players of contact sports?

83
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:45,950
Q10. What do longevity statistics reveal about the average person?

84
00:10:45,950 --> 00:11:05,110
Q11. What bonus does the keep moving principle bring us according to the speaker?

85
00:11:05,110 --> 00:11:13,030
PASSAGE 2
In 2014, one in sixteen Americans visited the hospital emergency room for home injuries.

86
00:11:13,030 --> 00:11:15,830
One of the main causes of these accidents?

87
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:16,790
A wandering mind!

88
00:11:16,790 --> 00:11:23,550
By one estimate, people daydream through nearly half of their waking hours.

89
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:31,010
Psychologists have recently focused on the tendency to think about something other than the task one is doing.

90
00:11:31,010 --> 00:11:38,870
For one experiment, researchers developed an app to analyze the relationship between daydreaming and happiness.

91
00:11:38,870 --> 00:11:52,370
They found that the average person’s mind wandered most frequently about 65% of the time during personal activities, such as brushing their teeth and combing their hair.

92
00:11:52,370 --> 00:11:59,290
Respondents’ minds tended to wander more when they felt upset rather than happy.

93
00:11:59,290 --> 00:12:05,990
They were more likely to wander toward pleasant topics than unpleasant ones.

94
00:12:05,990 --> 00:12:08,750
How do daydreams affect daydreamers?

95
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:12,170
A wandering mind leaves us vulnerable when driving.

96
00:12:12,170 --> 00:12:24,710
In one study, researchers interview 955 people involved in traffic accidents, the majority of them reported having daydream just before the accident.

97
00:12:24,710 --> 00:12:29,670
Yet other research suggests that daydreaming has benefits.

98
00:12:29,670 --> 00:12:36,010
Researches have found that it gives us a chance to think about our goals and it also seems to increase creativity.

99
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:50,350
In one experiment, 145 undergraduates completed four unusual uses tasks, each requiring them to list as many uses as possible for everyday object.

100
00:12:50,350 --> 00:13:00,530
After the first pair of tasks was completed, one group of participants was assigned an undemanding activity intended to cause their minds to wander.

101
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:18,070
When all the participants proceeded to the second pair of tasks, the daydreamers performed 40% better than the others.

102
00:13:18,070 --> 00:13:36,290
Q12. What does the passage say about people’s mind?

103
00:13:36,290 --> 00:13:55,010
Q13. For what purpose did the researchers develop the new app?

104
00:13:55,010 --> 00:14:14,510
Q14. How does daydreaming benefit people according to some researchers?

105
00:14:14,510 --> 00:15:01,770
Q15. What was the finding of the experiment with 145 undergraduates?

106
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:10,630
RECORDING 1
Dating from as far back as the 12th century, they are claimed to be the rarest historic buildings in western Europe.

107
00:15:10,630 --> 00:15:16,890
These buildings offer vital insight into Scandinavia’s Viking past.

108
00:15:16,890 --> 00:15:27,830
But now, with only 30 wooden churches remaining and their condition deteriorating, experts are working to preserve the structures for future generations.

109
00:15:27,830 --> 00:15:37,590
Some of these spectacular churches are no more than small buildings, barely 4 meters wide and 6 meters tall.

110
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:39,750
Others are much larger structures.

111
00:15:39,750 --> 00:15:43,310
They soar up to 40 meters into the cold air.

112
00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:48,290
Most consist of timber frames that rest on stone blocks.

113
00:15:48,290 --> 00:15:52,390
This means that they have no foundations.

114
00:15:52,390 --> 00:16:03,790
Although many of the churches appear from the outside to be complex structures, they normally feature only a single storey but numerous different roof levels.

115
00:16:03,790 --> 00:16:11,950
Staff from the Norwegian government have carried out conservation work on 10 of the churches over the past two years.

116
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:16,730
Most of these churches date from between the 12th and 14th centuries.

117
00:16:16,730 --> 00:16:19,610
Other churches were conserved in previous years.

118
00:16:19,610 --> 00:16:26,970
So far, specialists have worked to add preservative materials to the churches’ exteriors.

119
00:16:26,970 --> 00:16:33,630
They also replaced rotting roots and halted the sinking of the churches into the ground.

120
00:16:33,630 --> 00:16:41,690
In two cases, huge machines have been used to lift the buildings up to 30 centimeters into the air.

121
00:16:41,690 --> 00:16:48,110
This was accomplished so that the team could examine and repair the churches’ original medieval stone blocks.

122
00:16:48,110 --> 00:16:56,490
The team plans to return to around a dozen of the buildings to assess progress and consider further action.

123
00:16:56,490 --> 00:17:02,850
The earliest free-standing wooden church was probably built in Norway in around 1080.

124
00:17:02,850 --> 00:17:08,690
However, the largest known wooden churches were built from the 1130s onwards.

125
00:17:08,690 --> 00:17:19,390
This period was one of inter-elite rivalry, in which nobles sought to increase their influence by funding the construction of churches and other buildings.

126
00:17:19,390 --> 00:17:32,670
The reason for constructing the buildings from wood is probably that ideally proportioned straight and slender timber was available in large quantities in Scandinavia’s vast pine forests.

127
00:17:32,670 --> 00:17:39,850
As wood was so plentiful, it was cheaper to use than the stone used in the buildings of other European cultures.

128
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:51,450
The area’s ship building tradition, partly established by the Vikings, also meant that sophisticated carpentry was a major aspect of the local culture.

129
00:17:51,450 --> 00:18:10,330
The complex style of the medieval wooden church carvings and the skills used to make them almost certainly to derive from the ancient Viking tradition.

130
00:18:10,330 --> 00:18:29,390
Q16. What does the speaker say about the Viking wooden churches?

131
00:18:29,390 --> 00:18:48,750
Q17. What is special about most of the Viking wooden churches?

132
00:18:48,750 --> 00:19:06,910
Q18. Why were the Viking churches constructed from wood?

133
00:19:06,910 --> 00:19:14,610
RECORDING 2
In last week’s lecture, we discussed the characteristics of the newly born offspring of several mammals.

134
00:19:14,610 --> 00:19:23,070
You probably remember that human infants are less developed physically than other mammals of the same age.

135
00:19:23,070 --> 00:19:33,090
But in today’s lecture, we’ll look at three very interesting studies that hint at surprising abilities of human babies.

136
00:19:33,090 --> 00:19:39,250
In the first study, three-year-olds watch two videos shown side by side.

137
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:46,230
Each featuring a different researcher, one of whom they’d met once two years earlier.

138
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:52,410
The children spend longer watching the video showing the researcher they hadn’t met.

139
00:19:52,410 --> 00:20:00,510
This is consistent with young children’s usual tendency to look longer at things that aren’t familiar.

140
00:20:00,510 --> 00:20:03,230
And really this is amazing.

141
00:20:03,230 --> 00:20:11,790
It suggests the children remember the researcher they’ve met just one time when they were only one-year-olds.

142
00:20:11,790 --> 00:20:24,210
Of course, as most of us forget memories from our first few years as we grow older, this early long-term memories will likely be lost in subsequent years.

143
00:20:24,210 --> 00:20:26,810
Our second study is about music.

144
00:20:26,810 --> 00:20:34,710
For this study researchers play music to babies through speakers located on either side of a human face.

145
00:20:34,710 --> 00:20:40,510
They waited until the babies got bored and inverted their gaze from the face.

146
00:20:40,510 --> 00:20:47,530
And then they change the mood in the music either from sad to happy or the other way around.

147
00:20:47,530 --> 00:21:00,470
This mood switch made no difference to the three-month-olds; but for the nine-month-olds, it was enough to renew their interest and they started looking again in the direction of the face.

148
00:21:00,470 --> 00:21:09,690
This suggests that babies of that age can tell the difference between a happy melody and a sad tune.

149
00:21:09,690 --> 00:21:15,450
Our final study is from 1980, but it’s still relevant today.

150
00:21:15,450 --> 00:21:23,010
In fact, it’s one of the most famous pieces of research about infant emotion ever published.

151
00:21:23,010 --> 00:21:29,730
The study involved ordinary adults watching video clips of babies nine months or younger.

152
00:21:29,730 --> 00:21:43,110
In the video clips, the babies made various facial expressions in response to real life events including playful interactions and painful ones.

153
00:21:43,110 --> 00:21:50,110
The adult observers were able to reliably discern an assortment of emotions on the babies faces.

154
00:21:50,110 --> 00:22:01,450
These emotions included interest, joy, surprise, sadness, anger, disgust, contempt, and fear.

155
00:22:01,450 --> 00:22:06,170
Next week, we’ll be looking at this last study more closely.

156
00:22:06,170 --> 00:22:11,290
In fact, we will be viewing some of the video clips from that study.

157
00:22:11,290 --> 00:22:25,490
And together, see how well we do in discerning the babies’ emotions.

158
00:22:25,490 --> 00:22:43,170
Q19. What are the three interesting studies about?

159
00:22:43,170 --> 00:23:03,110
Q20. What does the second study find about nine-month-old babies?

160
00:23:03,110 --> 00:23:20,990
Q21. What is the 1980 study about?

161
00:23:20,990 --> 00:23:26,570
RECORDING 3
Today I’d like to talk about the dangers of being too collaborative.

162
00:23:26,570 --> 00:23:30,690
Being a good team player is a central skill in our modern workplace.

163
00:23:30,690 --> 00:23:39,450
The ability to work well with others and collaborate on projects is a sought-after ability in nearly every position.

164
00:23:39,450 --> 00:23:49,150
However, placing too much emphasis on being a good team player can negatively affect your career growth.

165
00:23:49,150 --> 00:23:53,030
Don’t be overly focused on gaining consensus.

166
00:23:53,030 --> 00:23:55,610
Don’t be too concerned with the opinions of others.

167
00:23:55,610 --> 00:24:05,470
This can hinder your ability to make decisions, speak up and gain recognition for your individual skills and strengths.

168
00:24:05,470 --> 00:24:20,590
Some people are too subordinate to others’ opinions, too focused on decision consensus, too silent about their own point of view, too agreeable to take things on when they don’t have time or energy.

169
00:24:20,590 --> 00:24:32,650
This leads to building a brand of underconfident, submissive, low-impact non-leaders and hampers their growth and career advancement.

170
00:24:32,650 --> 00:24:40,450
Collaboration certainly makes your individual competencies and contributions more difficult for outsiders to identify.

171
00:24:40,450 --> 00:24:44,950
Collaborative projects mean you’re sharing the spotlight with others.

172
00:24:44,950 --> 00:24:51,330
Outsiders may then find it difficult to determine your contributions and strengths.

173
00:24:51,330 --> 00:24:56,270
This may end up costing you opportunities for promotions or pay raises.

174
00:24:56,270 --> 00:25:03,870
You certainly shouldn’t ditch teamwork, but how can you avoid the hazards of being an over-collaborator?

175
00:25:03,870 --> 00:25:07,410
Working in a team can have huge benefits.

176
00:25:07,410 --> 00:25:12,750
Your team may have repeated successes and often gain recognition.

177
00:25:12,750 --> 00:25:18,610
You then have more opportunities to expand your professional network than if you worked alone.

178
00:25:18,610 --> 00:25:32,470
However, finding a balance between team efforts and individual projects that give you independent recognition is important for making a name for yourself and providing opportunities for advancement.

179
00:25:32,470 --> 00:25:35,570
Be selective in who you work with.

180
00:25:35,570 --> 00:25:40,910
This will maximize the benefits and minimize the downsides of being a team player.

181
00:25:40,910 --> 00:25:45,550
Collaborate with people who have complementary expertise.

182
00:25:45,550 --> 00:25:50,270
Select projects where there’s potential for mutual benefit.

183
00:25:50,270 --> 00:26:01,210
Perhaps you’re bringing your unique knowledge and gaining access to someone else’s professional network, or maybe you’re able to learn a new skill by working with someone.

184
00:26:01,210 --> 00:26:07,450
Seek out your teammates purposefully rather than jumping on every new group project opportunity.

185
00:26:07,450 --> 00:26:14,830
When we’re too collaborative, we want everyone to agree with a decision before we proceed.

186
00:26:14,830 --> 00:26:21,690
This can create unnecessary delays as you hold meetings trying to achieve consensus.

187
00:26:21,690 --> 00:26:25,150
It’s fine to be collaborative when seeking input.

188
00:26:25,150 --> 00:26:42,050
But put a deadline on the input stage and arrive at a decision, even if it’s a decision that doesn’t have consensus.

189
00:26:42,050 --> 00:27:00,230
Q22. What does the speaker say about being over collaborative?

190
00:27:00,230 --> 00:27:21,530
Q23. What does the speaker say about people engaged in collaborative projects?

191
00:27:21,530 --> 00:27:39,890
Q24. How do people benefit from working in a team?

192
00:27:39,890 --> 00:28:07,040
Q25. Why is it undesirable to spend too much time trying to reach consensus?
